  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAnother cool night expected tonight, with some wind gusts to 25\nmph possible towards central Maine this evening and tonight.\n\nStrong high pressure anchored across the Mid Atlantic will \ncontinue to bring dry conditions to the area along with a \nwarming trend. The presence of the high is strong enough to \nkeep avenues of moisture out of the region, as well as deflect \nany disturbances arriving from the west. These are mostly arced \ninto the Northern Plains and Great Lakes, before passing well \nnorth of the area through James Bay and central Quebec.\n\nThe result is continued dry weather with mostly sunny and clear\nskies and warming temperatures. ECMWF and GFS guidance suggests\nthe incoming ridge through late week will bring anomalously \nwarm 850mb temperatures, around the 90th percentile. Mixed down,\nsurface temperatures will run 10 to 15 degrees above normal \nhighs as they rise into the low to mid 60s. This stretch has \nfairly strong record highs (low to mid 70s) and think it would \nbe tough to topple those values.\n\nNot as warm into the weekend as the high moves directly\noverhead, but will need to see where warm air advection may take\nus as weather systems approach from the south and west early\nnext week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs broad ridging exits the East Coast late this weekend, \nmoisture will be quick to advance north. Guidance continues to\ndevelop low pressure off the SC/GA/FL coast with building return\nflow and SW advection from an incoming trough wicking it \nnorthward. Ensemble guidance is mostly in agreement that\nrain will move into NH and ME during the Monday timeframe. There\nremains differences in timing and amounts at this range.\n\nGreater ensemble IVT probabilities remain off the coast in the \nGulf of Maine as the system approaches. There may be a brief\nwindow of phasing with incoming trough from the west, but for\nthe most part deeper moisture fails to penetrate inland for\nfoothill/mountain locations. For now this keeps the chance for\ngreater accumulations towards the coastal communities. \n\nOverall storm trajectory and thus impacts will depend on the \nlow's proximity to the mainland as it nears the Gulf of Maine. \nAnd this may be influenced by the incoming trough to the west as\nwell as how far east or west the low develops in the South. EPS\nlow tracks have a wide zonal spread here, while GEFS is more \ncompact. It stands that confidence in rain amounts as well as\nany possible wind impacts will improve once the low takes shape\ninto this weekend.",
